# Degree-zero principal divisors for separable extensions of K(t)

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.hasPrincipalDivisors_of_finiteDimensional_of_isSeparable`
(`232eae2d-946e-5911-8a07-beaaf516642b`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `d54a6a76-b64d-59ad-9655-103d0ec4f2f6`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field and let $E$ be a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure and a
$\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$-algebra structure which are compatible, in the sense that $K \to
\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K \to E$ is a scalar tower; assume $E$ is finite-dimensional over the rational
function field $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$ and that the extension $E/\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$ is separable.
The conclusion is the class `HasPrincipalDivisors K E`, that is: for every $f \in E$ with $f \neq 0$
there exists a divisor $D$, meaning a finitely supported function from the places of $E$ over $K$ to
$\mathbb{Z}$, such that $D\,v = v.\mathrm{ord}\,f$ for every place $v$, and such that $D$ has degree
$0$, the degree being $\sum_{v \in \operatorname{supp} D} D\,v \cdot \deg v$. Here a place of $E$
over $K$ is a valuation subring of $E$ which contains the image of $K$ under the structure map, is
not the whole of $E$, and is a principal ideal ring. Since $D$ is required to be finitely supported
and to agree with $v \mapsto v.\mathrm{ord}\,f$ at every place, the assertion includes the statement
that $f$ has nonzero order at only finitely many places.

This is the degree-zero property of principal divisors for a global function field presented as a
finite separable extension of $K(t)$, the basic ingredient in the divisor-class theory of curves
over $K$. It is the form of the result used to deduce the same statement for an arbitrary separable
extension generated by a transcendental element, in
[`AlgebraicCurve.hasPrincipalDivisors_of_transcendental_of_isSeparable`]
(thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.hasPrincipalDivisors_of_transcendental_of_isSeparable);
the proof reduces to the rational function field case together with the conservation of degrees in
the fibre of a place, via the sum of ramification indices times inertia degrees.

## Explanation of the source proof

